• dfsin is not ready for real hardware simulation yet, so I restored the fallback in main.go:544 and put the unit test back in sim_internal_test.go:174. The tree is stable again:
  go test ./cmd/mygo -count=1 and go test ./internal/ir ./internal/mlir -count=1 both pass.

  What I verified before restoring it:

  - Removing the fallback did send dfsin through real Verilator. The run showed make: Entering directory / make: Leaving directory.
  - The hardware result was wrong immediately. First it produced garbage numeric outputs because native float64 ops were being lowered as plain 64-bit integer ops.
  - After patching that path experimentally, the next blocker was structural: looping soft-float helpers like float64_div could not be inlined cleanly.
  - Falling back to helper-module calls then hit backend MLIR issues around helper params and local array/register reads, so the compile still failed before simulation completed.

  The concrete blockers are:

  - Native float64 arithmetic/comparison/conversion lowering is incomplete for dfsin.
  - The alternative helper-module path is also incomplete in the MLIR/backend pipeline for this workload.

  So the expected outcome you asked for was not achievable safely yet. I did not regenerate simulation.result.txt, because with the stable code it remains on fallback, and with
  fallback removed the hardware path still fails for real compiler/backend reasons.

  If you want, I can take the next pass specifically on one of these two directions:

  1. add proper native float64 lowering in the IR builder, or
  2. finish the modular helper-call path so looping soft-float helpers like float64_div can compile.


